WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:14.650
Hi.

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:17.290
I'm Regina Herzlinger.

00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:18.440
Call me Reggie.

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:20.750
Everybody calls me Reggie.

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:24.320
I'm a professor at the Harvard Business School

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:29.100
and I've been teaching a course on how to innovate

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:33.260
in health care for about 30 years.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:37.520
I've had thousands of graduates from all over the world.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:39.420
I'm so proud of them.

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:47.890
Many of them have innovated health care, but there's still so much opportunity.

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:50.960
So health care is gigantic.

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:54.120
It's about $3 trillion in the US.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.342
So what is $3 trillion?

00:00:56.342 --> 00:00:59.360
Who can imagine what that is?

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:04.170
So that's the size of the GDP of China.

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:07.550
That's just the US health care system.

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:10.720
And the rest of the world is just about as big.

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:13.451
So $6 trillion.

00:01:13.451 --> 00:01:17.270
$6 trillion of waste.

00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:22.330
It is among the most wasteful sectors in the world.

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:27.460
In fact, health care has negative labor productivity.

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:33.390
What that means in English is every extra dollar or euro that you stick

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:37.260
into health care diminishes the GDP.

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:40.060
Certainly not what we want.

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:42.410
Then its quality.

00:01:42.410 --> 00:01:43.810
Who knows?

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:46.130
Totally reputational.

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:49.490
So when you go to the store and you buy a yogurt,

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:55.190
you take this little package of yogurt and you know so much about the yogurt.

00:01:55.190 --> 00:01:57.355
First of all, you know its price.

00:01:57.355 --> 00:02:01.070
Do you know the price of anything in the health care?

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:02.710
Very much doubt it.

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:06.490
Secondly, you know a lot about the quality of the yogurt.

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:09.820
How much calcium does it have, how many calories

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:14.000
does it have, how much protein does it have.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.560
So you know pretty much what you want to know about the yogurt.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:21.250
How about when you go to your doctor?

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:25.820
How about if you're really sick and you need a mastectomy or if you're a guy

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:28.520
and you need a prostatectomy?

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:29.950
What do you know?

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:31.470
You don't know anything.

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:33.570
So quality is a problem.

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:39.250
Don't get me wrong, we've got great doctors, we've got great hospitals,

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:41.840
we've got great technology.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:45.040
But how do we know how great they are?

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:47.380
Everybody can't be great.

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:50.660
So that's another problem in health care.

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:52.550
And isn't it ironic?

00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:58.310
I know everything I want to know about a trivial purchase like my yogurt,

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:03.530
but when it comes to my health care, I don't know anything.

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:05.970
Third problem is access.

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:11.330
So even in the United States, the richest country in the world,

00:03:11.330 --> 00:03:18.060
currently, as I speak, out of our 330 million people,

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:23.350
we have 50 million people who don't have health insurance.

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:27.550
And even if our health care reform law kicks in,

00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:31.920
we'll still have 20 million people without health insurance.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:35.470
All over the world, people lack health insurance.

00:03:35.470 --> 00:03:42.270
In fact, the Chinese are great savers and every good thing

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:45.380
has a bad thing associated with it.

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:50.920
Good thing, they save, bad thing, they don't spend their money.

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:56.580
Most economies rely on consumption to keep the economy going.

00:03:56.580 --> 00:04:00.920
And when the Chinese don't spend money but save money,

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:04.210
ironically, they dampen the economy.

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:07.020
So why are they such great savers?

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:09.680
They don't have health insurance.

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:16.050
And they're worried that if they get sick, how will they pay for it?

00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:18.269
So they save.

00:04:18.269 --> 00:04:21.630
So we have a lot of uninsured.

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:27.720
Many developing countries have virtually no health insurance.

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:33.110
And even countries that allegedly have health insurance

00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:35.970
don't really have health insurance.

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:41.480
So if you're in the UK, everybody has health insurance.

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:45.630
Does everybody have equal access to care?

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:46.480
No.

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:50.210
If you're poor, if you're not assertive, if you

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:54.290
don't look like you're going to sue, if you're not connected,

00:04:54.290 --> 00:04:58.570
if your uncle or your aunt isn't a doctor or a nurse,

00:04:58.570 --> 00:05:00.940
you go to the back of the line.

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:04.880
Now, I've got to give the people in the UK a lot of credit.

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:07.510
They keep studying this problem.

00:05:07.510 --> 00:05:12.070
Most governments would bury it and say no problem.

00:05:12.070 --> 00:05:13.830
But they keep studying it.

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:19.830
And decade after decade, just the mere fact of having insurance

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:23.540
doesn't mean you have access.

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:27.490
So lots of opportunities.

00:05:27.490 --> 00:05:31.270
We've got to control the costs of health care.

00:05:31.270 --> 00:05:35.250
Virtually every developed country in the world,

00:05:35.250 --> 00:05:41.920
as the GDP grows like this, the cost of health care grows like that.

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:45.580
And because it has negative productivity,

00:05:45.580 --> 00:05:48.930
that's not good for the GDP.

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:52.300
We've got issues with quality.

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:55.350
Namely, we don't know how good it is.

00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:59.080
And then thirdly, even with countries that

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:04.360
have health insurance for everybody, doesn't really mean everybody.

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:07.740
It means if you look like you're going to sue,

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:10.890
if you know somebody in the health care system,

00:06:10.890 --> 00:06:15.840
you get different kind of treatment from people who don't know.

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:20.720
These are all problems, but they are all opportunities.

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:24.720
Opportunities to make health care cheaper.

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:30.850
Opportunities to standardize and measure the quality of health care.

00:06:30.850 --> 00:06:35.470
Opportunities to increase access so that everybody

00:06:35.470 --> 00:06:38.750
can access the same quality of care.

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:44.620
So I hear about people with ideas about how to make it better.

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:48.750
Day in, day out, tons of ideas.

00:06:48.750 --> 00:06:54.280
Telemedicine, new drugs, new devices, new computer programs,

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:57.190
new ways of delivering care.

00:06:57.190 --> 00:07:01.620
Tablets so that people who have elder loved ones

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:04.860
can write down all the information.

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:07.950
Concierge medicine.

00:07:07.950 --> 00:07:09.790
Kinds of offering.

00:07:09.790 --> 00:07:14.300
So if you need an appointment, you don't have to stand on the line forever

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:18.420
on the phone, you can do it via your PC.

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:20.630
Ideas, ideas, ideas.

00:07:20.630 --> 00:07:21.930
But you know what?

00:07:21.930 --> 00:07:27.230
Most of these ideas never come to fruition.

00:07:27.230 --> 00:07:33.800
And if they do, many of them, all too many of them, fail.

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:36.200
So here's what we're going to do.

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:44.690
We're going to look at a way of analyzing whether an innovation is

00:07:44.690 --> 00:07:50.270
likely to succeed or likely to fail.

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:55.490
And we're going to do this with an analytic framework.

00:07:55.490 --> 00:08:03.030
And then once we've determined whether an innovation is likely to succeed,

00:08:03.030 --> 00:08:08.820
we're going to look at the elements of the business model that

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:12.000
may give it a really viable business.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:15.850
Now, when I say business, I don't mean for profit.

00:08:15.850 --> 00:08:20.660
I mean any kind of entrepreneurial venture,

00:08:20.660 --> 00:08:26.160
whether it's for profit, nonprofit, governmental, we're going to say,

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:28.600
does this thing have legs?

00:08:28.600 --> 00:08:31.930
We're going to separate the wheat from the chaff.

00:08:31.930 --> 00:08:34.799
And then once we've got the wheat there, we're

00:08:34.799 --> 00:08:39.169
going to look at the elements that make it successful.

00:08:39.169 --> 00:08:46.600
So I have a framework that has nine steps in it.

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:52.760
And we're going to systematically go through these nine steps.

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:58.750
And then, if you hang in there with me, and more power to you if you do,

00:08:58.750 --> 00:09:01.520
we're going to go through a number of case

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:07.590
studies of different kinds of entrepreneurial health care ventures.

00:09:07.590 --> 00:09:12.990
And we're going to keep applying this framework, this analytic framework,

00:09:12.990 --> 00:09:15.610
can we tell the winners from the losers?

00:09:15.610 --> 00:09:20.880
And from the winners, what are the key elements that need to be there

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:24.750
in order to make them successful?

00:09:24.750 --> 00:09:29.120
So we're going to study this with these different case studies.

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:33.600
And they're global, they're all entrepreneurial,

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:38.150
they cover different kinds of innovations.

00:09:38.150 --> 00:09:46.880
And then we have videos of these CEOs of these companies, these people who

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:53.240
are the key movers and shakers, and we've interviewed them.

00:09:53.240 --> 00:09:57.430
And you're going to learn how they got to where they got.

00:09:57.430 --> 00:09:59.890
What makes for an entrepreneur?

00:09:59.890 --> 00:10:06.510
And when you see these people, you'll see they look like everybody else.

00:10:06.510 --> 00:10:10.110
So what is it that made them an entrepreneur?

00:10:10.110 --> 00:10:14.520
And then secondly, in their sector, what do they

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:19.710
think are the next great ideas for innovation?

00:10:19.710 --> 00:10:22.190
I pay a lot of attention to this.

00:10:22.190 --> 00:10:27.050
These are not only smart people, but they walk the talk.

00:10:27.050 --> 00:10:28.470
They've really done it.

00:10:28.470 --> 00:10:33.440
So every case study says, what should here or she do?

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:36.540
What should this entrepreneur do?

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:39.970
We're going to explore what we think they should do

00:10:39.970 --> 00:10:43.990
and they're going to tell us what they actually did.

